.TH SDE-SYSTEM-MONITOR 1 "October 2026" "SDE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sde-system-monitor \- processes, performance graphs and file systems for SDE
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sde-system-monitor
[\fB\-\-page\fR \fIprocesses\fR|\fIperformance\fR|\fIdisks\fR|\fIstartup\fR]
.br
.B sde-system-monitor
\fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
SDE System Monitor is the native task manager of the Selviana Desktop
Environment. It reads /proc and /sys directly from a background thread once a
second and only repaints what changed.
.TP
.B Processes
\fBMy processes\fR (the default): your apps (processes that own a window or
were started from a .desktop file, shown with their name and icon) and your
background processes, the programs and services that run as you.
\fBAll processes\fR adds the system's processes (those of root and of the
other users). Either view can be a parent/child tree. Columns: name, PID,
user, CPU, memory, disk read and write rates, threads and state; the list is
sorted by CPU until you click another column header. The search field
filters by name, user, PID or command line.
End Task, Kill, Stop, Continue, Change Priority, Open File Location and
Properties are in the context menu and the Actions button. Acting on another
user's process asks for administrator rights through
.BR pkexec (1).
.IP
The threads of the Linux kernel (kworker, ksoftirqd, rcu_preempt,
migration/0 ...) are hidden; the line under the list says how many.
\fBShow kernel threads\fR lists them in a group of their own, \fBLinux
kernel\fR, each with what it does ("Linux kernel \(em worker thread"); the
tooltip and the line under the list explain the selected one. kworker
threads are the kernel's workers: they do the work that drivers, file
systems and the network hand over, such as writing to a disk. Kernel
threads belong to no program, use no memory of their own and cannot be
ended, so End Task, Kill, Stop, Continue and Change Priority are not
offered for them. The view and the kernel threads switch are kept in the
[system-monitor] section of ~/.config/sde/sde.conf.
.TP
.B Performance
Live 60 second graphs for the processor (total or per core, with model, speed,
load average and up time), memory and swap, every disk (read and write
throughput, active time), every network interface (receive and send), AMD GPU
utilization when the driver exposes it, and the battery.
.TP
.B File Systems
Mounted local storage with type, usage bar and free space. Open shows the
mount point in Liria.
.TP
.B Startup Apps
XDG autostart entries that SDE starts at login. Click a row to switch it; the
change is written to ~/.config/autostart, never to the system directories.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-page " " \fINAME\fR
Open on a page: processes, performance, disks (also filesystems) or startup.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH KEYBOARD
Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+4 switch pages, Ctrl+F searches processes, Up and Down move the
selection, Enter opens Properties, the Menu key opens the context menu,
Ctrl+W or Ctrl+Q closes the window.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sde/sde.conf
Theme, accent color and UI scale; in [system-monitor], processes=mine|all
and kernel-threads=true|false.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/
Per-user autostart entries and overrides.
